  "summary": "New preprints show that splits, negative sets and confidence scores can misstate the performance of co-folding, peptide and interaction models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether reported performance reflects the biological property of interest. For protein-ligand co-folding, confidence scores barely beat random selection, and the proposed CORE Score instead uses structural agreement among predictions. For antimicrobial peptides, cluster-disjoint versus random splitting and negative-set choice change results for frozen ESM-2 classifiers. A DrugBank-derived drug-drug interaction benchmark with four chemical-overlap holdout regimes shows that molecular representation rankings depend on the split. A knowledge-graph study addresses topological hub bias with Inverse Node Frequency normalisation. The published PertiNet and MeSH-knowledge-graph DDI studies are primarily model-development reports; the latter gives AUROC 0.988 transductive and 0.937 in a second setting. What is new is the emphasis on split design and reliability estimation over headline accuracy.",
